passages.text:
**Record.** The name is everywhere and the man is nowhere:

- VANE TIN CO on the crates in the Flooded Gallery and in the Deep Level at BlackPine;
- Vane Manor, with its hall clock stopped at 11:47;
- Vane Sawmill at BlackPine;
- the will, which names "whoever remains when the last lantern goes out".

**Canon.** Lord Aldous Vane came from Port-au-Levain, the fog-bound island of the Sovereign Sourdough Council, reached by sea. He came inland and made his money in tin before the Line came. This year he went home to the island, coughing, and he died there. On the island, every telling opens with Vane already dead.

The news reached HollowMere by wire or by rumour before any ferry could have carried it. In HollowMere he is "three nights" dead, and his will is read here.

**Ruling.** He died on the island, and he is entered as dead in HollowMere. Both are entered. On the island, he is a death the place remembers. Inland, he is a name and a will.

*Not in the clerk's hand. Typed on three slips and pasted in behind Entry XVII. The type is French in its spelling of the island and English in everything else. The slips are later than the book, and they speak of a century before it.*

**Record.** A rock in the North Atlantic, off New France, beyond the reef. It is held by a Guild chartered under Letters Patent of the Crown in 1787. Its clock is the Morning Ferry. Every soul there has a Wake Loaf under a cloche on a silver platter in the Hall of Platters, and the platter nearest the Hearth is gilded. The Hall's great south doors open onto Gallows Square.

**Canon.** Four keep the Hall, by colour:
- **the Gallery**, the Council houses, in gold leaf: order, rank, the Patent;
- **the Guild**, the trades, in flour-white: whoever pays;
- **the Commons**, the dish pit, the dockhands and the unregistered, in a red crust: bread, then the Hall;
- **the Abbey of the Crust**, in burlap: the Mother's purity, and the keys.
